<section class="excard" id="ex-a" aria-labelledby="ex-a-h">
<h3 id="ex-a-h">Pair A · From the learning trail's accelerometer goal</h3>
<dl>
<dt>How I learned and confirmed it:</dt>
<dd>Learned from the CST286 accelerometer learning trail and PhET Forces and Motion; confirmed by pushing a 0.20 kg object with 0.30 N on the frictionless ice setting and seeing the acceleration read 1.5 m/s&sup2;.</dd>
<dt>My relationship:</dt>
<dd>Acceleration equals push divided by mass, <code>a = F / m</code>, where <code>F</code> is the push force in newtons (N), <code>m</code> is the phone's mass in kilograms (kg), and <code>a</code> is the acceleration in meters per second squared (m/s&sup2;).</dd>
<dt>My condition:</dt>
<dd>My push is the only horizontal force acting on the phone (friction is negligible).</dd>
</dl>
<p class="exsub">Scenario 1 (Applies)</p>
<dl>
<dt>The situation:</dt>
<dd>My phone lies flat on a smooth icy tray, and I give it a steady sideways push across the ice.</dd>
<dt>Given:</dt>
<dd>Push force <code>F = 0.30 N</code>, phone mass <code>m = 0.20 kg</code>.</dd>
<dt>Worked:</dt>
<dd><code>a = F / m = 0.30 N &divide; 0.20 kg = 1.5 m/s&sup2;</code> in the direction of the push.</dd>
<dt>Why the condition holds:</dt>
<dd>On smooth ice, friction against the tray is close to zero, so my push is the only horizontal force on the phone.</dd>
</dl>
<p class="exsub nonapp">Scenario 4 (Looks similar, does not apply)</p>
<dl>
<dt>The situation:</dt>
<dd>I place the same phone on a wooden desk and give it the exact same sideways push of 0.30 N.</dd>
<dt>Looks like scenario 1 because:</dt>
<dd>The phone's mass (0.20 kg) and my sideways push (0.30 N) are identical.</dd>
<dt>The subtle difference, and the part of my condition it breaks:</dt>
<dd>The wooden desk rubs against the phone with sliding friction, which breaks the condition that my push is the only horizontal force acting on the phone.</dd>
<dt>What my relationship would get wrong here:</dt>
<dd>Using <code>a = F / m</code> with 0.30 N predicts 1.5 m/s&sup2;, which is too large (or the phone may not move at all), because friction opposes the push and reduces the net force on the phone.</dd>
</dl>
</section>

<section class="excard" id="ex-b" aria-labelledby="ex-b-h">
<h3 id="ex-b-h">Pair B · From a thrown ball (Example D on the goal form)</h3>
<dl>
<dt>How I learned and confirmed it:</dt>
<dd>Learned from OpenStax College Physics Section 3.4 (Projectile Motion) and checked in the PhET Projectile Motion lab by firing a baseball at 20 m/s at 30&deg; with air resistance off, which landed at 35.3 m.</dd>
<dt>My relationship:</dt>
<dd>Horizontal distance equals release speed squared times the sine of twice the launch angle, divided by gravity: <code>R = (v&sup2; &middot; sin(2&theta;)) / g</code>, where <code>v</code> is release speed in meters per second (m/s), <code>&theta;</code> is launch angle above horizontal in degrees (&deg;), <code>g = 9.8 m/s&sup2;</code>, and <code>R</code> is horizontal landing distance in meters (m).</dd>
<dt>My condition:</dt>
<dd>The ball lands at the same height it was released from, and air resistance is small enough to ignore compared to gravity.</dd>
</dl>
<p class="exsub">Scenario 1 (Applies)</p>
<dl>
<dt>The situation:</dt>
<dd>A fielder throws a baseball across a flat field at 20 m/s at an angle of 30&deg; above horizontal, and another player catches it at the same shoulder height.</dd>
<dt>Given:</dt>
<dd>Release speed <code>v = 20 m/s</code>, launch angle <code>&theta; = 30&deg;</code>, gravity <code>g = 9.8 m/s&sup2;</code>.</dd>
<dt>Worked:</dt>
<dd><code>R = ((20 m/s)&sup2; &middot; sin(60&deg;)) &divide; 9.8 m/s&sup2; = (400 m&sup2;/s&sup2; &middot; 0.866) &divide; 9.8 m/s&sup2; = 346.4 m&sup2;/s&sup2; &divide; 9.8 m/s&sup2; = 35.3 m</code>.</dd>
<dt>Why the condition holds:</dt>
<dd>The ball is caught at the same height it was thrown, and a baseball is heavy and compact enough over a 2-second throw that air drag is tiny compared to gravity.</dd>
</dl>
<p class="exsub nonapp">Scenario 4 (Looks similar, does not apply)</p>
<dl>
<dt>The situation:</dt>
<dd>A player hits a badminton shuttlecock across a flat court at the same initial speed of 20 m/s and angle of 30&deg;, caught at the same height.</dd>
<dt>Looks like scenario 1 because:</dt>
<dd>The launch speed (20 m/s), launch angle (30&deg;), and landing height are identical to the baseball throw.</dd>
<dt>The subtle difference, and the part of my condition it breaks:</dt>
<dd>The shuttlecock is very light (5 grams) with a wide feathered skirt that catches the air, which breaks the condition that air resistance is small enough to ignore.</dd>
<dt>What my relationship would get wrong here:</dt>
<dd>The formula predicts the shuttlecock will travel 35.3 m, which is far too large; in reality air drag slows the shuttlecock dramatically in the first second so it drops within a few meters.</dd>
</dl>
